## The Grade Floor at the $10K Tier

The grade floor changes at $10K. At $5K, the floor was G-VS2 Excellent cut GIA. At $10K, the lab path moves that floor up.

**E color minimum for lab 3ct.** At 9.4mm face-up, E color is colorless in every setting. G is correct at 1ct (6.5mm). At 3ct, the stone is significantly more visible and the grade difference reads more clearly. E or D is the right choice.

**VVS1 clarity minimum.** At 3ct, VS2 inclusions are more visible under real-world light. They sit closer to the surface than at 1ct. VVS1 means inclusions are microscopic — not detectable without a loupe. For a stone this size, do not go below VVS1.

**Excellent or Ideal cut only.** This applies at every budget. A Very Good cut at 3ct produces visible dark spots. The light performance difference between Excellent and Very Good at 9.4mm is significant. All 4 picks in this guide use Excellent or Ideal cut stones.

**IGI or GIA for lab.** IGI is the standard for lab-grown diamonds. It is accurate for lab stones. GIA also certifies lab-grown diamonds, and the GIA cert carries more institutional weight. Pick 4 uses a GIA-certified lab 3ct. It is the only pick where the cert issuer is GIA. See [Round Cut Diamond](/round-cut-diamond) for how to compare IGI and GIA grade reports side by side.

