### Pick 4 — GIA Lab + Platinum: $8,866

**Setting:** [Diagonal Pavé in Platinum — $1,526](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/diagonal-lined-with-pave-diamond-edge-mens-wedding-ring-in-platinum-1-8-ct-tw-item-196082?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Stone:** [GIA D-VVS1 3ct Excellent Cut Lab-Grown Round — $7,340](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26775736?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Total: $8,866**

This is the premium pick. The only GIA-certified lab stone in this guide. The only platinum setting. GIA D-VVS1 at 3ct is the highest-graded lab stone in this budget. GIA lab certs are rarer than IGI ones.

Why GIA matters: it grades lab diamonds on the same scale as natural diamonds. Same color grades. Same clarity grades. IGI is accurate for lab-grown. But GIA carries institutional credibility that every appraiser and dealer recognizes. If the cert issuer matters to you, GIA is the right call.

Platinum never needs rhodium plating — unlike [white gold](/mens-white-gold-diamond-ring), it stays naturally white. The diagonal pavé platinum band has diamond accents set diagonally across the band edge. It is the same band from Pick 5 in the under-$5,000 guide. Here it carries a 3ct D-VVS1 lab stone, not a 1ct D-VS2 natural. The stone is 44% more face-up diameter. The cert is GIA.

At $8,866, this is $1,846 over Pick 1. You are paying for three upgrades: GIA cert, D color, and platinum. All three are real. All three are verifiable. If those credentials matter to you — and to the people around you — this is the correct pick.

What you give up: commodity resale. Lab diamonds, including GIA-certified lab stones, do not retain value at resale. If the ring is a visual statement and not a financial asset, that trade is rational.

**Verdict:** Best pick for men who want the strongest credentials in this guide. GIA cert, D-VVS1 clarity, platinum durability. At $8,866, it is the most credentialed lab statement ring available under $10,000.

